<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Zheng</h1><p class="st-meta">806 BCE – 375 BCE · capital Xinzheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The commercial heart of the Central Plain, whose Duke Zhuang humbled the Zhou king and whose minister Zichan published China's first written law code.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 806 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-xuan-of-zhou">King Xuan of Zhou</a> enfeoffed his younger brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-zheng">Duke Huan of Zheng</a>, in the Wei valley. Foreseeing the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a>, Huan moved his family and wealth east; his son Duke Wu conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-guo">Eastern Guo</a> and Kuai and built a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinzheng">Xinzheng</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-zhuang-of-zheng">Duke Zhuang</a> made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zheng-wang-shichong">Zheng</a> the leading state of the early Spring and Autumn period, and in 707 BCE his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> wounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huan-of-zhou">King Huan of Zhou</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xuge">Xuge</a>, shattering royal prestige. Zheng had a strong merchant class bound to the state by covenant. In 536 BCE the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zichan">Zichan</a> had the penal laws cast on bronze tripods, making law public for the first time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucians</a> condemned the music of Zheng as licentious.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Caught between Jin and Chu, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zheng-wang-shichong">Zheng</a> was a constant battlefield. After the partition of Jin, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-han">State of Han</a> conquered it in 375 BCE and moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinzheng">Xinzheng</a>, which remained the Han capital until 230 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-zheng.jpg" alt="State of Zheng" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>806 BCE – 375 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xinzheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Huan of Zheng, Duke Zhuang of Zheng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zheng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=state-of-zheng" aria-label="State of Zheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7469 930 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Zheng lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7789 1186L7768 1193M7789 1186L7891 1187M7789 1186L7823 1175"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7891 1187h0M7823 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7789 1186h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7789 1186h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-590&amp;focus=state-of-zheng">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

